# 3. Pass 2 — Panchang and festival-date validation

**Verdict: Essential, but this section needs the most careful revision.**

The plan proposes a benchmark covering 2026–2027 across Delhi, Varanasi, Mumbai, Chennai, and Kolkata, with seven major festival families and boundary tests. implementation_plan

This is a useful starting point, but it is not sufficient by itself to establish that the app is universally accurate.

## A. Clarify the astronomical reference standard

Your plan specifies Drik Ganita with Chitrapaksha Lahiri Ayanamsa and describes it as the official standard of the Indian Astronomical Ephemeris. implementation_plan

There is an important distinction here.

The Indian Meteorological Department's Positional Astronomy Centre publishes the Indian Astronomical Ephemeris and Rashtriya Panchang, which provide astronomical and calendrical data for Panchang makers. The Rashtriya Panchang also documents its calculation conventions and geographical reference basis. [India Meteorological Department](https://mausam.imd.gov.in/responsive/indianAstronomicalEphemeris.php?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

However, that does **not** establish that every regional festival-date rule, every Drik Panchang configuration, and every regional tradition produces identical observance dates.

I recommend changing the plan from:

> Drik Ganita with Chitrapaksha Lahiri Ayanamsa is the single official standard for every regional observance.

To:

> Use a documented Drik Ganita calculation convention with a specified Lahiri/Chitrapaksha Ayanamsa implementation as the astronomical baseline. Validate astronomical values against authoritative ephemeris data, and validate festival observance dates against explicitly identified regional rules and independent calendar references. Record and explain any differences.

This is more defensible technically and better for user trust.

## B. Separate astronomical calculations from religious observance rules

These are two related but different validation problems.

**Astronomical validation** checks values such as:
- Solar and lunar positions.
- Tithi boundaries.
- Nakshatra boundaries.
- Sunrise and sunset.
- Moonrise and moonset.
- Relevant astronomical transitions.

**Festival-rule validation** checks how those values determine a particular observance:
- Which Tithi must prevail at a specific time.
- Whether sunrise, Pradosh, Nishita, or moonrise is the deciding window.
- Whether a regional convention changes the result.
- Whether Smartha and Vaishnava observances differ.
- Whether a rule concerning Bhadra or another boundary applies.

For example, calculating a Tithi transition accurately does not, on its own, establish the correct observance date for every Janmashtami tradition.

**Required change:** maintain separate tests and separate evidence for the astronomical engine and the festival-date evaluator.

## C. Strengthen the reference dataset

Do not put expected values directly into test assertions without documenting their provenance.

For each reference case, record:

| Field | Required information |
|---|---|
| Reference ID | Stable identifier |
| Festival | Standard festival name |
| Region or tradition | The convention being tested |
| City | Reference location |
| Date | Expected observance date |
| Timing | Expected start/end or astronomical event |
| Reference source | Identifiable source or publication |
| Reference version | Edition, URL, or other traceable identifier |
| Retrieved or checked on | Date of verification |
| Tolerance | Acceptable difference for the particular value |
| Notes | Reason for any discrepancy |

This makes the test results auditable and helps prevent circular testing.

Where an independent reference reports a date but not the underlying event times, use it to validate the date—not to claim that every underlying astronomical calculation is independently verified.

## D. Keep the five-city benchmark, but expand it sensibly

The five proposed locations provide useful geographical coverage. implementation_plan

I would retain them for the initial suite, then expand testing to:
- Locations near relevant date or timing boundaries.
- Additional cities selected to exercise different latitudes and longitudes.
- Dates around lunar-month transitions.
- Observances whose rules differ by tradition.

Do not claim that five cities establish accuracy for every coordinate in India.

Also, a strict zero-day tolerance is reasonable for a date that has an unambiguous, documented expected result under a specified convention. It is not a valid universal criterion when two references are using different regional observance rules.

For timings, define an appropriate tolerance separately. Do not use the festival-date tolerance for all astronomical values.

## E. Revise the boundary tests

The proposed tests for Tithi transitions near sunrise, midnight crossover, Adhika Masa, leap day, and year boundaries are worthwhile. implementation_plan

Add tests for:

- Events crossing midnight without changing their applicable local date incorrectly.
- Date and timezone conversion.
- Invalid coordinates and out-of-range inputs.
- Missing or inconsistent ephemeris values.
- Daylight-saving transitions for any supported location outside India.
- Month and year boundaries in both the astronomical engine and festival evaluator.
- Regional-rule differences for festivals where they matter.
- Reproducibility of results for the same inputs.

Most importantly, **do not adjust the calculation engine merely to make it match one website**. Investigate discrepancies, identify the differing convention, and document the resolution.

